[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is Go Proxy, a reverse proxy built specifically for self-hosters and its pitch fits in one sentence. Stop configuring your proxy. Run a container with one Docker label and it\'s live on its own subdomain with HTTPS automatically.'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Idle services go to sleep and wake on the next request. There\'s a web UI with metrics, logs, and uptime for the whole box. And if you\'re currently running Nginx Proxy Manager, like this channel'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is, this video is the exact comparison you\'ve been looking for. The honest catch list comes at the end, as always. The velocity first, 163 releases in 2 and 1/2 years with version 0.30.3'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shipping just yesterday and the repository pushed again this morning. Fun detail from the GIT history. The first commit landed on February 29th, 2024. A project born on a leap day'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shipping like it\'s making up for all the birthdays it\'s going to miss. It\'s built by Userion, using on GitHub, a developer in Hong Kong who has personally landed 885 of the project\'s roughly 2,200'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commits. This is the classic home lab project origin born from scratching a personal itch shaped in the open by a Discord full of self-hosters filing feedback and shipped at a weekly pace that most'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'funded teams honestly can\'t match. Here\'s the web UI and this is where Go Proxy stops being just a proxy. A dashboard with all your services and their uptime, CPU, memory, and disk per node. Live logs streaming again,'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'container controls right there. It\'s half reverse proxy, half home lab control panel. The overview that Nginx Proxy Manager users currently assemble from three separate tools and a bookmark folder. There\'s even a public demo'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instance of this exact interface linked below so you can click around before committing to anything. The feature list, fast. Automatic route discovery for Docker and Podman. Containers appear as routes the moment they start and'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'vanish when they stop. Automatic HTTPS through Let\'s Encrypt with DNS 01 wildcard certificates, TCP and UDP forwarding, OpenID Connect single sign-on and'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'forward auth for putting real login in front of anything, middlewares, custom error pages, access control by IP, CIDR, country, or even time zone. And the crowd favorite, idle sleep services'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nobody is using get stopped then woken on the next request. And the same trick works on Proxmox LXC containers, which Go Doki can start, stop, and stream logs'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from directly. Setup is a script and a compose file. Point a wildcard DNS record at your machine, run the setup script, docker compose up, and open the web UI on your own domain. From that moment on, every new service you deploy'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is one label. Proxy.aliases, the subdomain you want. And Go Doki does the rest. That is genuinely the whole workflow forever. Compare that with a click choreography you memorize in NPM,'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you\'ll feel the difference on the very first deploy. Now, the comparison many of you and honestly, this channel itself this week came here for Go Doki versus Nginx Proxy Manager.'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Credit first, NPM is a fantastic on-ramp. A friendly UI over Nginx, click to add a proxy host, click again for a certificate. It\'s how half the home lab'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'world learned reverse proxying. Ours included. The cost shows up as you grow. Every service means manual clicks, the config lives in an internal database you can\'t version, and the project\'s release'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pace has slowed. Go Doki inverts the model completely. Your compose files become the source of truth. Roots appear and disappear with their containers. Certificates are wildcard automatic, and'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the web UI is for observing your home lab, not configuring it. So, should you actually switch? Honest answers because we\'re mid-migration ourselves. Your NPM proxy hosts do not import automatically.'

[03:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'ll redeclare each service as a Docker label. It\'s boring, but it\'s mechanical and you can run both proxies in parallel on different ports while you move things over. Then cut DNS across when you\'re'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'confident. If your setup is three services that never change, NPM is fine. Genuinely stay. If you add and remove containers every week, labels beat Clicks decisively. And if you run Proxmox, the LXC integration'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'alone is worth the experiment. The daily life shift is really the whole argument. Before, with Clicks, deploy a container, open NPM, add a proxy host, pick the upstream end port, request a certificate'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at Ticaric and quietly forget to remove the host when the service dies 6 months later. After, with labels, the route lives in the same compose file as the service itself. Deploy together, version'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together in Git, gone together. Your proxy configuration can\'t drift out of sync with reality because it literally is the same file as reality. The documentation holds up on a phone, which matters when you\'re debugging from the'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'couch. Quick start, the full Docker label reference, DNS 01 provider guides for every registrar you\'d actually use, middleware docs, and a live public demo instance you can prod before installing'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a single thing. There\'s even a community-built GPT assistant for questions, which tells you something about how this community likes to operate. The numbers. 2 and 1/2 years in, 4,051 stars, 178 forks, nearly 2,200 commits.'

